WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.240
A report needs a consistent table state while ingestion keeps adding orders.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:08.530
Iceberg records successive states as snapshots.

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:15.330
A snapshot describes a complete state using file references; it is not merely a bag of newly inserted rows.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:21.960
In the original example, the first batch creates snapshot one with forty rows.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:26.245
The second creates a state with eighty rows and names the first as its parent.

00:00:26.245 --> 00:00:29.430
The third state contains one hundred and twenty rows.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:33.655
Older data files can be reused across these states without being modified.

00:00:35.155 --> 00:00:39.950
Reading an older snapshot selects its referenced inputs before the query runs.

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:44.155
Keeping a snapshot identifier in a report is not enough on its own.

00:00:44.155 --> 00:00:47.555
Its metadata and data files must remain available.

00:00:47.555 --> 00:00:53.640
Run the lesson, follow the parent identifiers, then explain which state the current count describes.
